y_1 = 5. Therefore, print No.\n- For the second question, the graph G^{{(2)}} is not good because it has a path 2 \\rightarrow 6 connecting vertices x_2 = 2 and y_2 = 6. Therefore, print No.\n- For the third question, the graph G^{{(3)}} is good. Therefore, print Yes.\n- For the fourth question, the graph G^{{(4)}} is good. Therefore, print Yes.\n\nAs seen in this sample input, note that the given graph G may have self-loops or multi-edges.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import defaultdict, deque\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    it = iter(data)\n    n = int(next(it)); m = int(next(it))\n    \n    graph = defaultdict(list)\n    for _ in range(m):\n        u = int(next(it)); v = int(next(it))\n        if u != v:\n            graph[u].append(v)\n            graph[v].append(u)\n    \n    k = int(next(it))\n    bad_pairs = []\n    for _ in range(k):\n        x = int(next(it)); y = int(next(it))\n        bad_pairs.append((x, y))\n    \n    # Find connected components\n    visited = [False] * (n + 1)\n    comp_id = [-1] * (n + 1)\n    comp_count = 0\n    \n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        if not visited[i]:\n            stack = [i]\n            visited[i] = True\n            while stack:\n                node = stack.pop()\n                comp_id[node] = comp_count\n                for neighbor in graph[node]:\n                    if not visited[neighbor]:\n                        visited[neighbor] = True\n                        stack.append(neighbor)\n            comp_count += 1\n    \n    # Precompute which bad pairs are in same component\n    bad_comp_pairs = set()\n    for x, y in bad_pairs:\n        cx, cy = comp_id[x], comp_id[y]\n        if cx > cy:\n            cx, cy = cy, cx\n        bad_comp_pairs.add((cx, cy))\n    \n    q = int(next(it))\n    out_lines = []\n    for _ in range(q):\n        p = int(next(it)); q_node = int(next(it))\n        cp = comp_id[p]; cq = comp_id[q_node]\n        if cp > cq:\n            cp, cq = cq, cp\n        # Check if adding edge (p,q) creates a path for any bad pair\n        if (cp, cq) in bad_comp_pairs:\n            out_lines.append(\"No\")\n        else:\n            out_lines.append(\"Yes\")\n    \n    print(\"\\n\".join(out_lines))\n\nmain()\n```",
