    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef main():\n    import sys\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    N = int(data[0])\n    A = int(data[1])\n    B = int(data[2])\n    D = list(map(int, data[3:3+N]))\n    \n    # The cycle length is W = A + B\n    W = A + B\n    \n    # For each plan day d_i, consider d_i mod W\n    # If d_i mod W is in [1, A] (1-indexed), then it's a holiday\n    # But since we don't know today's day, we need to check if there exists some offset k (0 <= k < W)\n    # such that for all i, (k + D_i) mod W is in [1, A]\n    \n    # Equivalently, for all i, (D_i + k) % W <= A and > 0\n    # => k % W in [(-D_i + 1) % W, (-D_i + A) % W] (circular interval)\n    \n    # Instead, transform D_i to r_i = D_i % W, but handle 0 specially\n    # Better: consider all possible residue classes mod W. However W can be huge.\n    \n    # Key insight: the condition holds iff max(D_i) - min(D_i) < W AND \n    # there exists some x such that all D_i mod W fall into [x+1, x+A] (mod W)\n    \n    # Actually, simpler: check if all D_i mod W can fit within some consecutive A days\n    # Compute residues: r_i = (D_i - 1) % W  (so 0-indexed days 0..W-1, holidays 0..A-1)\n    # Then we need a shift s such that all (r_i + s) % W in [0, A-1]\n    # => all r_i in [-s, -s + A - 1] mod W\n    \n    # Check if the maximum gap between consecutive residues (in circular order) >= B\n    # If yes, then there's a gap of at least B weekdays where no plan falls, so possible\n    \n    residues = sorted([(d - 1) % W for d in D])\n    \n    if not residues:\n        print(\"Yes\")\n        return\n    \n    # Add circular wrap-around gap\n    gaps = []\n    for i in range(len(residues)):\n        if i == len(residues) - 1:\n            gap = (residues[0] + W) - residues[i]\n        else:\n            gap = residues[i+1] - residues[i]\n        gaps.append(gap)\n    \n    if max(gaps) >= B:\n        print(\"Yes\")\n    else:\n        print(\"No\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```",
