    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nIn the country of AtCoder, there are N stations: station 1, station 2, \\ldots, station N.\nYou are given M pieces of information about trains in the country. The i-th piece of information (1\\leq i\\leq M) is represented by a tuple of six positive integers (l _ i,d _ i,k _ i,c _ i,A _ i,B _ i), which corresponds to the following information:\n\n- For each t=l _ i,l _ i+d _ i,l _ i+2d _ i,\\ldots,l _ i+(k _ i-1)d _ i, there is a train as follows:\n- The train departs from station A _ i at time t and arrives at station B _ i at time t+c _ i.\n\n\n\nNo trains exist other than those described by this information, and it is impossible to move from one station to another by any means other than by train.\nAlso, assume that the time required for transfers is negligible.\nLet f(S) be the latest time at which one can arrive at station N from station S.\nMore precisely, f(S) is defined as the maximum value of t for which there is a sequence of tuples of four integers \\big((t _ i,c _ i,A _ i,B _ i)\\big) _ {{i=1,2,\\ldots,k}} that satisfies all of the following conditions:\n\n- t\\leq t _ 1\n- A _ 1=S,B _ k=N\n- B _ i=A _ {{i+1}} for all 1\\leq i\\lt k, \n- For all 1\\leq i\\leq k, there is a train that departs from station A _ i at time t _ i and arrives at station B _ i at time t _ i+c _ i.\n- t _ i+c _ i\\leq t _ {{i+1}} for all 1\\leq i\\lt k.\n\nIf no such t exists, set f(S)=-\\infty.\nFind f(1),f(2),\\ldots,f(N-1).\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN M\nl _ 1 d _ 1 k _ 1 c _ 1 A _ 1 B _ 1\nl _ 2 d _ 2 k _ 2 c _ 2 A _ 2 B _ 2\n\\vdots\nl _ M d _ M k _ M c _ M A _ M B _ M\n\nOutput\n\nPrint N-1 lines.\nThe k-th line should contain f(k) if f(k)\\neq-\\infty, and Unreachable if f(k)=-\\infty.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 2\\leq N\\leq2\\times10 ^ 5\n- 1\\leq M\\leq2\\times10 ^ 5\n- 1\\leq l _ i,d _ i,k _ i,c _ i\\leq10 ^ 9\\ (1\\leq i\\leq M)\n- 1\\leq A _ i,B _ i\\leq N\\ (1\\leq i\\leq M)\n- A _ i\\neq B _ i\\ (1\\leq i\\leq M)\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n6 7\n10 5 10 3 1 3\n13 5 10 2 3 4\n15 5 10 7 4 6\n3 10 2 4 2 5\n7 10 2 3 5 6\n5 3 18 2 2 3\n6 3 20 4 2 1\n\nSample Output 1\n\n55\n56\n58\n60\n17\n\nThe following diagram shows the trains running in the country (information about arrival and departure times is omitted).\n\nConsider the latest time at which one can arrive at station 6 from station 2.\nAs shown in the following diagram, one can arrive at station 6 by departing from station 2 at time 56 and moving as station 2\\rightarrow station 3\\rightarrow station 4\\rightarrow station 6.\n\nIt is impossible to depart from station 2 after time 56 and arrive at station 6, so f(2)=56.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n5 5\n1000000000 1000000000 1000000000 1000000000 1 5\n5 9 2 6 2 3\n10 4 1 6 2 3\n1 1 1 1 3 5\n3 1 4 1 5 1\n\nSample Output 2\n\n1000000000000000000\nUnreachable\n1\nUnreachable\n\nThere is a train that departs from station 1 at time 10 ^ {{18}} and arrives at station 5 at time 10 ^ {{18}}+10 ^ 9. There are no trains departing from station 1 after that time, so f(1)=10 ^ {{18}}.\nAs seen here, the answer may not fit within a 32\\operatorname{{bit}} integer.\nAlso, both the second and third pieces of information guarantee that there is a train that departs from station 2 at time 14 and arrives at station 3 at time 20.\nAs seen here, some trains may appear in multiple pieces of information.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n16 20\n4018 9698 2850 3026 8 11\n2310 7571 7732 1862 13 14\n2440 2121 20 1849 11 16\n2560 5115 190 3655 5 16\n1936 6664 39 8822 4 16\n7597 8325 20 7576 12 5\n5396 1088 540 7765 15 1\n3226 88 6988 2504 13 5\n1838 7490 63 4098 8 3\n1456 5042 4 2815 14 7\n3762 6803 5054 6994 10 9\n9526 6001 61 8025 7 8\n5176 6747 107 3403 1 5\n2014 5533 2031 8127 8 11\n8102 5878 58 9548 9 10\n3788 174 3088 5950 3 13\n7778 5389 100 9003 10 15\n556 9425 9458 109 3 11\n5725 7937 10 3282 2 9\n6951 7211 8590 1994 15 12\n\nSample Output 3\n\n720358\n77158\n540926\n255168\n969295\nUnreachable\n369586\n466218\n343148\n541289\n42739\n165772\n618082\n16582\n591828",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nimport heapq\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().strip().split('\\n')\n    if not data:\n        return\n    \n    n, m = map(int, data[0].split())\n    \n    # For each station, store outgoing edges as (departure_time, arrival_time, destination)\n    # But since times are large and trains are periodic, we need to process efficiently\n    \n    # Group edges by (A, B) and store their parameters\n    # Then, for each edge (A->B), we want to know the latest possible departure time from A to reach B\n    # such that the arrival at B <= current best time at B\n    \n    # We'll do a reverse Dijkstra-like algorithm from station N backwards\n    # dist[i] = latest time to reach station N from station i\n    # Initialize dist[N] = infinity, others = -infinity\n    \n    # For efficiency, for each edge (A, B, l, d, k, c), the departure times are:\n    # t = l, l+d, ..., l+(k-1)*d, arriving at t+c\n    \n    # When processing in reverse, we want for each edge A->B, given that we know dist[B],\n    # what's the latest departure time from A via this edge that allows reaching B in time <= dist[B]\n    \n    # That is, find the largest t in the arithmetic progression <= dist[B] - c\n    \n    # Store edges grouped by source station for forward pass, but for reverse Dijkstra,\n    # we need for each edge (A,B), when we update dist[B], we can potentially update dist[A]\n    \n    # So store edges in both directions? No, store by destination for reverse propagation\n    \n    # Let's store edges indexed by destination: for each node v, list of (u, l, d, k, c) such that there's an edge u->v\n    edges_to = [[] for _ in range(n+1)]\n    \n    idx = 1\n    for i in range(m):\n        parts = list(map(int, data[idx].split()))\n        l, d, k, c, a, b = parts\n        idx += 1\n        edges_to[b].append((a, l, d, k, c))\n    \n    # dist[i] = latest time to reach station N from station i\n    # Initialize with -10**30 (as -infinity) for all except station N\n    INF = 10**30\n    NEG_INF = -INF\n    dist = [NEG_INF] * (n+1)\n    dist[n] = 10**30  # Set a very large number for station N (infinity)\n    \n    # We'll use a priority queue (max-heap) by time, but heapq is min-heap, so store negative\n    # Or store (-time, node) and pop smallest negative -> largest time\n    heap = []\n    # Start with station N having \"arrival time\" = INF (meaning we can arrive at any time before INF)\n    heapq.heappush(heap, (-10**30, n))  # We want to process larger times first, so use negative\n    \n    # For efficiency, we'll use the standard Dijkstra approach but relax edges in reverse\n    # When processing a node v with current best dist[v], for each incoming edge (u -> v),\n    # we compute the latest departure time from u via this edge that allows reaching v in time <= dist[v]\n    \n    while heap:\n        neg_time, v = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        cur_dist_v = -neg_time\n        \n        # If we have a better (larger) time for v already, skip\n        if cur_dist_v < dist[v]:\n            continue\n        \n        # Process all edges that go TO v (i.e., edges from u to v)\n        for (u, l, d, k, c) in edges_to[v]:\n            # The latest arrival time at v is dist[v] (if infinite, treat as very large)\n            # So latest departure time from u is dist[v] - c\n            latest_departure = dist[v] - c\n            \n            # Find the largest t in {l, l+d, ..., l+(k-1)*d} that is <= latest_departure\n            # Solve l + (x)*d <= latest_departure, x in [0, k-1], maximize t = l + x*d\n            # x <= (latest_departure - l) // d\n            # max_x = min(k-1, (latest_departure - l) // d)\n            # if (latest_departure - l) < 0, then no valid departure\n            \n            if latest_departure < l:\n                continue\n                \n            max_x = min(k-1, (latest_departure - l) // d)\n            t_dep = l + max_x * d\n            \n            # t_dep is the latest departure time from u via this edge that reaches v in time <= dist[v]\n            # Then arrival at v is t_dep + c, which must be <= dist[v]\n            \n            # This gives us a way to reach N from u: depart at t_dep, reach v, then go to N\n            # So candidate time for u is t_dep\n            \n            if t_dep > dist[u]:\n                dist[u] = t_dep\n                heapq.heappush(heap, (-t_dep, u))\n    \n    # Output f(1) to f(N-1)\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        if dist[i] == NEG_INF:\n            print(\"Unreachable\")\n        else:\n            print(dist[i])\n\nmain()\n```",
