    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_243",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nWe have N dice.\r\nFor each i = 1, 2, \\ldots, N, when the i-th die is thrown, it shows a random integer between 1 and A_i, inclusive, with equal probability.\nFind the probability, modulo 998244353, that the following condition is satisfied when the N dice are thrown simultaneously.\n\nThere is a way to choose some (possibly all) of the N dice so that the sum of their results is 10.\n\n How to find a probability modulo 998244353\nIt can be proved that the sought probability is always a rational number. Additionally, the constraints of this problem guarantee that if the sought probability is represented as an irreducible fraction \\frac{{y}}{{x}}, then x is not divisible by 998244353. Here, there is a unique integer z such that xz \\equiv y \\pmod{{998244353}}. Report this z.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN\r\nA_1 A_2 \\ldots A_N\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the answer.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1 \\leq N \\leq 100\n- 1 \\leq A_i \\leq 10^6\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n4\r\n1 7 2 9\n\nSample Output 1\n\n942786334\r\n\nFor instance, if the first, second, third, and fourth dice show 1, 3, 2, and 7, respectively, these results satisfy the condition.\r\nIn fact, if the second and fourth dice are chosen, the sum of their results is 3 + 7 = 10.\r\nAlternatively, if the first, third, and fourth dice are chosen, the sum of their results is 1 + 2 + 7 = 10.\nOn the other hand, if the first, second, third, and fourth dice show 1, 6, 1, and 5, respectively, there is no way to choose some of them so that the sum of their results is 10, so the condition is not satisfied.\nIn this sample input, the probability of the results of the N dice satisfying the condition is \\frac{{11}}{{18}}.\r\nThus, print this value modulo 998244353, that is, 942786334.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n7\r\n1 10 100 1000 10000 100000 1000000\n\nSample Output 2\n\n996117877",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nMOD = 998244353\n\ndef modinv(a, m=MOD):\n    return pow(a, m - 2, m)\n\ndef solve():\n    import sys\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().split()\n    \n    n = int(data[0])\n    A = list(map(int, data[1:1+n]))\n    \n    # dp[mask] = probability that subset-sum mask (for sums up to 10) is achievable\n    # But better: dp[s] = probability that sum s can be achieved by some subset so far\n    # We only care about sums up to 10 (since target is 10)\n    # Use DP: dp[i][s] = whether sum s is achievable after considering first i dice? \n    # But probabilities: For each die, track which subset sums are possible with non-zero probability\n    \n    # Alternative: dp[s] = probability that sum s is achievable using some subset of processed dice\n    # Initialize: dp[0] = 1 (empty subset gives sum 0 with probability 1)\n    # For each die i with faces 1..A_i:\n    #   New ways to get sum s: either don't use die i (keep current dp[s]) or use die i (then need dp[s - face] before)\n    # But careful: we want existence of ANY subset with sum 10, not sum over subsets.\n    # So better: dp[s] = probability that sum s is achievable (i.e., at least one subset gives sum s)\n    \n    # However, inclusion-exclusion needed because events are not disjoint.\n    # Instead, compute complement: probability that NO subset sums to 10.\n    \n    # Alternate approach: DP over dice, for each die track which subset sums are possible.\n    # Since max sum we care about is 10, we can use bitset of size 11 (sums 0..10).\n    # Let dp[mask] = probability that the set of achievable sums (among 0..10) is exactly represented by mask.\n    # But there are 2^11 = 2048 states, feasible for n <= 100.\n    \n    # Represent sums 0..10 as bits in an 11-bit number. bit k means sum k is achievable.\n    # Start with state 1 (bit0 = 1), meaning only sum 0 achievable (by empty subset).\n    # For each die with face values v in 1..A_i, but note only v <= 10 matters (larger values can't help reach sum 10 when added to existing subset sum <=10).\n    # So for each die, consider only faces 1..min(A_i, 10). Faces >10 only affect via \"not using die\", but since we're tracking which sums are achievable, \n    # adding a die with large face doesn't add new small sums, only potentially keeps old sums.\n    \n    # Actually, for any die:\n    #   If we include it, new achievable sums = old sums \u222a {old_sum + v} for v in [1, min(A_i, 10)] (if v <=10)\n    #   If v > 10, including it won't change sums \u226410.\n    # So only faces \u226410 matter for achieving sums \u226410.\n    \n    # Let p_v = probability die shows v (for v=1..min(A_i,10)), and p_big = probability die shows >10.\n    # When die shows v\u226410: new_state = old_state OR (old_state << v)\n    # When die shows v>10: new_state = old_state (unchanged)\n    \n    # dp[new_state] += dp[old_state] * prob\n    \n    # Final answer = 1 - (probability that state does not include bit 10)\n    \n    total_states = 1 << 11  # sums 0..10\n    \n    dp = [0] * total_states\n    dp[1] = 1  # start with only sum 0 achievable (bit0 set)\n    \n    for a in A:\n        # Compute probabilities for die outcomes affecting sums <=10\n        # faces 1..min(a,10): each has prob 1/a; faces >10: count = max(0, a - 10), prob = max(0, a-10)/a\n        p_big = max(0, a - 10) / a if a > 10 else 0\n        probs_small = {}\n        for v in range(1, min(a, 10) + 1):\n            probs_small[v] = 1.0 / a\n        \n        new_dp = [0] * total_states\n        for state in range(total_states):\n            if dp[state] == 0:\n                continue\n            prob = dp[state]\n            \n            # Case 1: die shows big (>10): state unchanged\n            new_dp[state] = (new_dp[state] + prob * p_big) % MOD\n            \n            # Case 2: die shows v in 1..min(a,10)\n            for v, pv in probs_small.items():\n                if a > 10 and v == 10:\n                    # Avoid double counting: if a<=10, v=10 included in loop; if a>10, v=10 also included but prob=1/a\n                    pass\n                new_state = state | ((state << v) & 0x7FF)  # keep only bits 0..10\n                new_dp[new_state] = (new_dp[new_state] + prob * pv) % MOD\n        \n        dp = new_dp\n    \n    # Sum probabilities where bit10 is set (sum 10 achievable)\n    ans = 0\n    for state in range(total_states):\n        if state & (1 << 10):\n            ans = (ans + dp[state]) % MOD\n    \n    # Convert float to mod fraction: dp uses floats, but we need rational mod arithmetic\n    # Actually, above uses floats which is wrong. Need exact modular arithmetic.\n    \n    # Rework with fractions using modular inverses:\n    # Instead of /a, multiply by modinv(a, MOD)\n    \n    # Reset dp: dp[1] = 1 (as integer mod MOD)\n    dp = [0] * total_states\n    dp[1] = 1\n    \n    for a in A:\n        inv_a = modinv(a, MOD)\n        new_dp = [0] * total_states\n        \n        for state in range(total_states):\n            if dp[state] == 0:\n                continue\n            cur = dp[state]\n            \n            # Big faces (>10): count = max(0, a-10)\n            big_count = max(0, a - 10)\n            if big_count > 0:\n                new_dp[state] = (new_dp[state] + cur * big_count * inv_a) % MOD\n            \n            # Small faces: 1..min(a,10)\n            for v in range(1, min(a, 10) + 1):\n                new_state = state | ((state << v) & 0x7FF)\n                new_dp[new_state] = (new_dp[new_state] + cur * inv_a) % MOD\n        \n        dp = new_dp\n    \n    ans = 0\n    for state in range(total_states):\n        if state & (1 << 10):\n            ans = (ans + dp[state]) % MOD\n    \n    print(ans)\n\nsolve()\n```",
