      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef compute_ceil_division():\n    X = int(sys.stdin.readline().strip())\n    # For positive numbers: (X + 9) // 10 works for ceiling division\n    # For negative numbers: // operator already floors toward negative infinity\n    # To get ceiling for negative numbers, we can use:\n    # ceil(X/10) = -floor((-X)/10) when X is negative?\n    # Actually, we need a unified approach.\n    # Using math.ceil would be ideal, but let's avoid floating point for huge numbers\n    \n    # Alternative approach:\n    # For any integer X and positive integer d:\n    # ceil(X/d) = (X + d - 1) // d if X >= 0\n    # ceil(X/d) = X // d if X < 0 and divisible by d\n    # ceil(X/d) = X // d + 1 if X < 0 and not divisible by d\n    \n    # More unified formula: \n    # ceil(a/b) = (a + b - 1) // b when a >= 0, but fails for negative\n    # Another unified approach: (a + (b-1)) // b doesn't work for negatives\n    \n    # Let's handle both cases explicitly:\n    if X >= 0:\n        result = (X + 9) // 10\n    else:\n        # For negative numbers, // already gives floor (more negative)\n        # We want ceiling, which is floor + 1 unless divisible by 10\n        if X % 10 == 0:\n            result = X // 10\n        else:\n            result = X // 10 + 1\n    \n    print(result)\n\ncompute_ceil_division()\n```",
