hing not allowing immediate win? But Vova wants to delay win.\n        #   However, after Vova's move, it's Vanya's second turn. Can Vanya force a win later?\n        #\n        # But note: after Vanya's first move: state is n\u00b11 \u2261 \u00b11 mod 3.\n        # Vova can either:\n        #   If Vanya did +1: state = n+1 \u2261 1 \u2192 Vova can do -1 \u2192 back to n (\u22610) or +1 \u2192 n+2 \u22612.\n        #   But Vova wants to avoid letting Vanya win. So if Vova chooses to go to n+2 (\u22612),\n        #   then on next move Vanya can subtract 1 to get n+1 (\u22611) \u2014 not divisible.\n        #   Or add 1 to get n+3 \u22610 \u2192 wins!\n        #\n        # Wait: if after Vova's move the number is x, then Vanya can win if x+1 or x-1 is divisible by 3.\n        # That is, if x \u2261 2 or x \u2261 1 mod 3 respectively.\n        # So if at any Vanya's turn the number is \u22611 or 2 mod 3, he can win immediately.\n        #\n        # Therefore, the only way Vova can prevent Vanya from winning is to ensure that at the start of every Vanya's turn, the number is divisible by 3.\n        #\n        # Initially: n \u22610 \u2192 Vanya moves to \u22611 or 2 \u2192 then Vova can move it back to \u22610 (by subtracting 1 from 1\u21920, or adding 1 to 2\u21920? no: 2+1=3\u22610, but 2-1=1\u22600).\n        # Actually:\n        #   If Vanya moves to \u22611, Vova can subtract 1 \u2192 \u22610.\n        #   If Vanya moves to \u22612, Vova can add 1 \u2192 \u22610? 2+1=3\u22610 \u2192 yes.\n        # So Vova can always return the number to \u22610 mod 3 after his move.\n        #\n        # Then at each of Vanya's turns, the number is \u22610 mod 3 \u2192 he cannot win.\n        # This continues for all 5 of Vanya's moves.\n        # After 10 moves, Vova wins.\n        #\n        # Therefore:\n        #   If n % 3 == 0 \u2192 Vova wins (\"Second\")\n        #   Else \u2192 Vanya wins on first move (\"First\")\n\n        if n % 3 == 0:\n            print(\"Second\")