           backtrack(player + 1, team_assign, current_teams)\n        \n        # Try creating a new team (if we haven't exceeded T teams)\n        if current_teams < T:\n            team_assign[player] = current_teams\n            backtrack(player + 1, team_assign, current_teams + 1)\n    \n    # But wait: this counts labeled assignments (since we're using team indices), but we need unlabeled\n    # Actually no: because we enforce that teams are created in order (only add new team when needed),\n    # this avoids overcounting due to team relabeling\n    # However, check the sample: N=5, T=2 -> should give 4\n    \n    # Let me test logic: first player goes to team 0. Second player can go to team 0 (if compatible) or team 1.\n    # This ensures we don't count permutations of teams separately.\n    \n    # But need to verify with sample: \n    # Players 0,1,2,3,4 (1-indexed 1,2,3,4,5)\n    # Incompatible: (0,2), (2,3) (since 1-3 -> 0-2, 3-4 -> 2-3)\n    \n    # Let's trace:\n    # Player 0 -> team 0\n    # Player 1: can go to team 0 (no conflict with 0) or team 1\n    #   team 0: \n    #     Player 2: cannot go to team 0 (incompatible with 0), so team 1\n    #       Player 3: cannot go to team 1 (incompatible with 2 who is in team 1), so try team 0? check with 0,1: no conflicts? \n    #          3 incompatible with 2 only, so ok for team 0. So assign to team 0.\n    #         Player 4: can go to either team (check conflicts: none with 0,1,3 in team 0; none with 2 in team 1)\n    #           so 2 ways here\n    #   team 1:\n    #     Player 2: cannot go to team 0 (conflict with 0), so team 1? but check: 2 incompatible with 0, but 0 is in team 0, so ok for team 1\n    #       Then same as above...\n    \n    # Hmm, might get more than expected. Let me re-think.\n    \n    # Alternative approach: iterate over all possible partitions of N players into T non-empty subsets,\n    # and for each partition, check if no incompatible pair is in same subset.\n    # Since N<=10, we can do this by generating all set partitions (Bell number for 10 is ~115975, manageable)\n    # But even better: use recursive assignment as above, but ensure we count correctly.\n    \n    # Actually, my backtracking approach above is standard for counting set partitions with constraints.\n    # The issue is: we want unlabeled teams, and my approach (only allowing new team at end) gives canonical labeling.\n    \n    # Let me compute for sample manually with code (but since I can't run, trust known method).\n    \n    # Standard way: \n    # ways = 0\n    # for each assignment f: [N] -> [T] surjective, check constraints\n    # but then divide by T! ? No, because teams are unlabeled, but problem says:\n    # \"two divisions are considered different when there are two players who belong to the same team in one division and different teams in the other\"\n    # This means: unlabeled teams! Because it's about the equivalence relation (same team).\n    \n    # So my backtracking should be correct (canonical labeling).\n    \n    # But let's verify count for sample input 1: \n    # Expected 4.\n    # My method: assign player 0 to team 0.\n    # Player 1: team 0 or 1.\n    # Case 1: player1->team0\n    #   player2: cannot go to team0 (incompatible with player0), so only team1.\n    #   player3: cannot go to team1 (incompatible with player2), so team0 (check: player3 vs player0? no; vs player1? no)\n    #   player4: can go to team0 or team1 (no constraints)\n    #      team0: {0,1,3,4}, {2} -> valid\n    #      team1: {0,1}, {2,4}, {3} -> wait no: player3 is in team0, so teams are 0 and 1, player4 can join either.\n    #        If join team0: {0,1,3,4}, {2}\n    #        If join team1: {0,1}, {2,4}, {3} -> but then we have 3 teams! But we require exactly T=2 teams.\n    #   Ah! Important: we must have exactly T teams, not <=T.\n    # So when player4 joins, we must end with exactly 2 teams.\n    #   In this case: if player4 joins team0, teams used: 2 (0 and 1) -> ok.\n    #   If player4 joins team1: still 2 teams (0 and 1) -> also ok.\n    #   So 2 ways here.\n    # Case 2: player1->team1\n    #   Now teams: 0: {0}, 1:{1}\n    #   player2: cannot join team0 (conflict with 0), so can join team1 (check conflict with 1? no) -> team1\n    #      Now: 0:{0}, 1:{1,2}\n    #      player3: cannot join team1 (conflict with 2), so join team0 -> ok.\n    #         player4: can join team0 or team1 -> 2 ways\n    #   Or player2 could create new team? But current_teams=2, T=2, so no. Only team1.\n    #   So 2 ways here.\n    # Total 4 -> matches.\n    \n    # So the algorithm is correct.\n    \n    team_assign = [-1] * N\n    backtrack(0, team_assign, 0)\n    print(count[0])\n\nsolve()\n```",
