    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nThere is a single integer N written on a blackboard.\r\nTakahashi will repeat the following series of operations until all integers not less than 2 are removed from the blackboard:\n\n- Choose one integer x not less than 2 written on the blackboard.\n- Erase one occurrence of x from the blackboard. Then, write two new integers \\left \\lfloor \\dfrac{{x}}{{2}} \\right\\rfloor and \\left\\lceil \\dfrac{{x}}{{2}} \\right\\rceil on the blackboard.\n- Takahashi must pay x yen to perform this series of operations.\n\nHere, \\lfloor a \\rfloor denotes the largest integer not greater than a, and \\lceil a \\rceil denotes the smallest integer not less than a.\nWhat is the total amount of money Takahashi will have paid when no more operations can be performed?\r\nIt can be proved that the total amount he will pay is constant regardless of the order in which the operations are performed.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the total amount of money Takahashi will have paid, in yen.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 2 \\leq N \\leq 10^{{17}}\n\nSample Input 1\n\n3\n\nSample Output 1\n\n5\r\n\nHere is an example of how Takahashi performs the operations:\n\n- Initially, there is one 3 written on the blackboard.\n- He chooses 3. He pays 3 yen, erases one 3 from the blackboard, and writes \\left \\lfloor \\dfrac{{3}}{{2}} \\right\\rfloor = 1 and \\left\\lceil \\dfrac{{3}}{{2}} \\right\\rceil = 2 on the blackboard.\n- There is one 2 and one 1 written on the blackboard.\n- He chooses 2. He pays 2 yen, erases one 2 from the blackboard, and writes \\left \\lfloor \\dfrac{{2}}{{2}} \\right\\rfloor = 1 and \\left\\lceil \\dfrac{{2}}{{2}} \\right\\rceil = 1 on the blackboard.\n- There are three 1s written on the blackboard.\n- Since all integers not less than 2 have been removed from the blackboard, the process is finished.\n\nTakahashi has paid a total of 3 + 2 = 5 yen for the entire process, so print 5.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n340\n\nSample Output 2\n\n2888\n\nSample Input 3\n\n100000000000000000\n\nSample Output 3\n\n5655884811924144128",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve():\n    import sys\n    n = int(sys.stdin.read().strip())\n    \n    # We observe that each split of x into floor(x/2) and ceil(x/2) costs x\n    # It's proven the total cost is independent of operation order\n    # We can think recursively: f(x) = x + f(floor(x/2)) + f(ceil(x/2))\n    # Base case: f(1) = 0 (no operations needed)\n    \n    # However, direct recursion won't work for N up to 10^17\n    # Let's find pattern by computing small values:\n    # f(1) = 0\n    # f(2) = 2 + f(1) + f(1) = 2\n    # f(3) = 3 + f(1) + f(2) = 3 + 0 + 2 = 5\n    # f(4) = 4 + f(2) + f(2) = 4 + 2 + 2 = 8\n    # f(5) = 5 + f(2) + f(3) = 5 + 2 + 5 = 12\n    \n    # Another approach: notice every time we split a number x > 1,\n    # we add x to the cost, and the total contribution comes from\n    # all internal nodes in the splitting tree (leaf nodes are 1s).\n    # The final state has exactly N ones (since we start with 1 number N,\n    # and splitting preserves sum: x becomes floor(x/2)+ceil(x/2)=x).\n    # So we have a binary tree with N leaves (all 1s) and internal nodes\n    # corresponding to splits.\n    #\n    # In such a full binary tree with N leaves, there are N-1 internal nodes.\n    # But the cost isn't just sum of all internal node values because the\n    # internal node values aren't all equal.\n    #\n    # Better approach: use memoization with dynamic programming and \n    # observe overlapping subproblems with same (low, high) ranges or \n    # use binary representation insight.\n    #\n    # Actually, let's compute cost differently:\n    # Each time we process a value x, we pay x, and we break it into halves.\n    # This forms a recursion where we can use bit manipulation.\n    #\n    # Insight: the total cost equals sum over all splits of the split value.\n    # Consider the binary expansion. For any number x, its \"contribution\" to\n    # the total cost is x times how many times it appears in the splitting process.\n    #\n    # Alternate solution from known problems: This is equivalent to computing\n    # the total internal path length of a certain binary tree, but there's \n    # a simpler formula.\n    #\n    # Let's define function f(n):\n    # f(1) = 0\n    # f(n) = n + f(n//2) + f((n+1)//2)   [since floor(n/2) = n//2, ceil(n/2) = (n+1)//2]\n    #\n    # We can use memoization, but n up to 1e17 requires optimization.\n    # Notice the recursive calls only hit O(log n) distinct values because\n    # n, n//2, (n+1)//2, n//4, (n//2+1)//2, etc. create at most 2*log2(n) distinct values.\n    \n    memo = {}\n    \n    def f(x):\n        if x <= 1:\n            return 0\n        if x in memo:\n            return memo[x]\n        \n        half1 = x // 2\n        half2 = (x + 1) // 2  # This equals x - half1\n        result = x + f(half1) + f(half2)\n        memo[x] = result\n        return result\n    \n    print(f(n))\n\nsolve()\n```",
