er is 2.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n1 2\r\n2\r\n1 2\n\nSample Output 2\n\n0\r\n\nS_1 already contains both 1 and M, so the minimum number of operations required is 0.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n3 5\r\n2\r\n1 3\r\n2\r\n2 4\r\n3\r\n2 4 5\n\nSample Output 3\n\n-1\n\nSample Input 4\n\n4 8\r\n3\r\n1 3 5\r\n2\r\n1 2\r\n3\r\n2 4 7\r\n4\r\n4 6 7 8\n\nSample Output 4\n\n2",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import defaultdict, deque\n\ndef main():\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().split()\n    \n    idx = 0\n    N = int(data[idx])\n    idx += 1\n    M = int(data[idx])\n    idx += 1\n    \n    sets = []\n    for _ in range(N):\n        A = int(data[idx])\n        idx += 1\n        s = list(map(int, data[idx:idx + A]))\n        idx += A\n        sets.append(set(s))\n    \n    # Check if any set already contains both 1 and M\n    for s in sets:\n        if 1 in s and M in s:\n            print(0)\n            return\n    \n    # Build graph: each set is a node, connect sets that have common elements\n    # We want to know if we can merge sets to get one containing 1 and M\n    graph = defaultdict(list)\n    element_to_sets = defaultdict(list)  # maps element -> list of set indices\n    \n    for i, s in enumerate(sets):\n        for elem in s:\n            element_to_sets[elem].append(i)\n    \n    # Union-Find to track connected components of sets\n    parent = list(range(N))\n    \n    def find(x):\n        if parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = find(parent[x])\n        return parent[x]\n    \n    def union(x, y):\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\n        if rx != ry:\n            parent[rx] = ry\n    \n    # For every element, union all sets containing it\n    for elem, set_indices in element_to_sets.items():\n        for i in range(1, len(set_indices)):\n            union(set_indices[0], set_indices[i])\n    \n    # Find which components contain sets with 1 and sets with M\n    comp_with_1 = set()\n    comp_with_M = set()\n    \n    for i, s in enumerate(sets):\n        root = find(i)\n        if 1 in s:\n            comp_with_1.add(root)\n        if M in s:\n            comp_with_M.add(root)\n    \n    # If no component has both 1 and M reachable through unions, impossible\n    if not (comp_with_1 & comp_with_M):\n        print(-1)\n        return\n    \n    # Now compute minimum operations\n    # Each merge reduces count of sets by 1\n    # We need to merge all sets in the final component that will contain both 1 and M\n    target_comp = next(iter(comp_with_1 & comp_with_M))  # pick one common component\n    \n    # Count how many sets belong to this component\n    sets_in_target = [i for i in range(N) if find(i) == target_comp]\n    num_sets_in_target = len(sets_in_target)\n    \n    # To merge k sets into 1, we need k-1 operations\n    print(num_sets_in_target - 1)\n\nmain()\n```",
