    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: \"A friend of yours says, \u201cEvery time I plan a vacation, there's always a crisis at home or work so that I can't go.\"\" Assuming that this is not really true, your friend's statement best illustrates which of the following?\"\n\nOptions: \nA. illusory correlation\nB. base rate fallacy\nC. cognitive dissonance\nD. anchoring bias\nE. availability heuristic\nF. fundamental attribution bias\nG. hindsight bias\nH. self-serving bias\nI. psychological reactance\nJ. confirmation bias\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The friend\u2019s statement reflects a pattern where they perceive a consistent link between planning a vacation and encountering a crisis\u2014even if no such real pattern exists\u2014due to remembering vivid, emotionally salient events (e.g., crises) more easily than mundane non-crisis times. This overestimation of co-occurrence due to memorable examples is characteristic of the **availability heuristic**, where people judge frequency or probability based on how easily examples come to mind. Although illusory correlation also involves perceiving a relationship where none exists, it typically emphasizes the pairing of two specific events (e.g., full moon and madness), whereas here the emphasis is on the ease of recall of crisis events interfering with vacation plans.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
