    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_71",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion:  Use indirect truth tables to determine whether each set of propositions is consistent. If the set is consistent, choose an option with a consistent valuation. (There may be other consistent valuations.)\n~N \u2261 (O \u00b7 P)\n~O \u2283 Q\nN \u00b7 ~Q\n\nOptions: \nA. Inconsistent\nB. Consistent. Consistent valuation when N, O, and P are true and Q is false\nC. Consistent. Consistent valuation when N and O are true and P and Q are false\nD. Consistent. Consistent valuation when N and P are true and O and Q are false\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We test consistency using the given propositions:  \n1. ~N \u2261 (O \u00b7 P)  \n2. ~O \u2283 Q  \n3. N \u00b7 ~Q  \n\nFrom (3), N must be true and Q must be false.  \nWith N = T, ~N = F, so (O \u00b7 P) must be F (since ~N \u2261 (O\u00b7P) requires both sides equal). So at least one of O or P is false.  \nWith Q = F, premise (2): ~O \u2283 Q becomes ~O \u2283 F. For this implication to be true, ~O must be false (since F \u2283 F = T, but T \u2283 F = F). So ~O = F \u21d2 O = T.  \nThus O = T, and from earlier, (O \u00b7 P) = F \u21d2 since O = T, P must be F.  \n\nSo assignment: N = T, O = T, P = F, Q = F satisfies all three statements.  \nThis matches option **C**: *N and O are true and P and Q are false*.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 234,
        "output_tokens": 237,
        "total_tokens": 471
