  {
    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_108",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Use indirect truth tables to determine whether the following argument is valid. If the argument is invalid, choose an option which presents a counterexample. (There may be other counterexamples as well.)\nH \u2261 (I \u00b7 ~J)\nK \u2261 ~H\n~(K \u2283 J) / I \u2261 J\n\nOptions: \nA. Valid\nB. Invalid. Counterexample when I, H, and K are true and J is false\nC. Invalid. Counterexample when I and H are true and J and K are false\nD. Invalid. Counterexample when K is true and I, H, and J are false\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We analyze the argument using indirect truth tables. Assume premises are true and conclusion false:  \nPremises:  \n1. $ H \\equiv (I \\cdot \\sim J) $  \n2. $ K \\equiv \\sim H $  \n3. $ \\sim(K \\supset J) $ \u2192 means $ K \\cdot \\sim J $  \nConclusion: $ I \\equiv J $ must be false \u2192 $ I $ and $ J $ have opposite truth values.\n\nFrom 3: $ K = T $, $ J = F $.  \nFrom 2: $ K = \\sim H $ \u21d2 $ H = F $.  \nFrom 1: $ H \\equiv (I \\cdot \\sim J) $, and since $ H = F $, $ (I \\cdot \\sim J) = F $.  \nBut $ J = F $, so $ \\sim J = T $, so $ I \\cdot T = I $; thus $ I = F $ for the conjunction to be false.  \nSo far: $ K=T, J=F, H=F, I=F $.  \nThen conclusion $ I \\equiv J $: $ F \\equiv F $ = true \u2014 contradicts needing it false.  \nTry $ I = T $ instead: then $ I \\cdot \\sim J = T \\cdot T = T $, but $ H = F $, so equivalence fails \u2014 invalid assumption.  \nWait \u2014 try different assignment: Option C: $ I=T, H=T, J=F, K=F $.  \nCheck premises:  \n1. $ H \\equiv (I \\cdot \\sim J) $: $ T \\equiv (T \\cdot T) = T $ \u2713  \n2. $ K \\equiv \\sim H $: $ F \\equiv \\sim T = F $ \u2713  \n3. $ \\sim(K \\supset J) $: $ K \\supset J = F \\supset F = T $, so negation is F \u2717 \u2014 not a counterexample.  \n\nOption B: $ I=T, H=T, K=T, J=F $  \n1. $ H \\equiv (I \\cdot \\sim J) $: $ T \\equiv (T \\cdot T) = T $ \u2713  \n2. $ K \\equiv \\sim H $: $ T \\equiv \\sim T = F $ \u2717 \u2014 fails.  \n\nOption D: $ K=T, I=F, H=F, J=F $  \n3. $ \\sim(K \\supset J) = \\sim(F) = T $ \u2713  \n2. $ K \\equiv \\sim H $: $ T \\equiv T $ \u2713  \n1. $ H \\equiv (I \\cdot \\sim J) $: $ F \\equiv (F \\cdot T) = F $ \u2713  \nConclusion $ I \\equiv J $: $ F \\equiv F = T $ \u2014 not false. So no invalidating assignment? Wait \u2014 recheck.  \nIf $ I=T, J=F $, then for conclusion false, need $ I \\neq J $. Try $ I=T, J=F $, then $ \\sim J = T $, so $ I \\cdot \\sim J = T $, so $ H=T $. Then $ K = \\sim H = F $. Then $ K \\supset J = F \\supset F = T $, so premise 3 fails.  \nOnly way premise 3 holds is $ K=T, J=F $. Then $ H=F $, and from 1: $ F \\equiv (I \\cdot T) $ \u21d2 $ I = F $. So $ I=F, J=F $ \u21d2 conclusion true. Thus argument **valid**.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 236,
        "output_tokens": 767,
        "total_tokens": 1003
      },
