    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_3",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Construct a complete truth table for the following argument. Then, using the truth table, determine whether the argument is valid or invalid. If the argument is invalid, choose an option which presents a counterexample. (There may be other counterexamples as well.)\n~G \u2283 H\n~H / G\n\nOptions: \nA. Valid\nB. Invalid. Counterexample when G and H are true\nC. Invalid. Counterexample when G is true and H is false\nD. Invalid. Counterexample when H is true and G is false\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The argument is:  \nPremise 1: ~G \u2283 H  \nPremise 2: ~H  \nConclusion: G  \n\nConstructing the truth table:\n\n| G | H | ~G | ~H | ~G \u2283 H | (Both premises T?) | Conclusion G |\n|---|---|----|----|--------|--------------------|--------------|\n| T | T | F  | F  |   T    |        No          |      T       |\n| T | F | F  | T  |   T    |        Yes         |      T       |\n| F | T | T  | F  |   T    |        No          |      F       |\n| F | F | T  | T  |   F    |        No          |      F       |\n\nOnly row 2 has both premises true (~G \u2283 H is true, ~H is true), and in that case, G is true. So the conclusion follows whenever the premises are true.\n\nTherefore, the argument is valid.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
