  {
    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_51",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion:  Which of the following propositions is an immediate (one-step) consequence in PL of the given premises?\n(N \u2283 ~O) \u2283 (O \u2228 Q)\nP \u2283 (N \u2283 ~O)\nN \u2283 P\n\nOptions: \nA. P\nB. N \u2283 ~O\nC. O \u2228 Q\nD. ~P\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "From the premises:  \n1. $(N \\supset \\sim O) \\supset (O \\vee Q)$  \n2. $P \\supset (N \\supset \\sim O)$  \n3. $N \\supset P$  \n\nUsing Hypothetical Syllogism on (2) and (3): $N \\supset P$ and $P \\supset (N \\supset \\sim O)$ give $N \\supset (N \\supset \\sim O)$.  \nSimplification (or contraction) yields $N \\supset \\sim O$.  \nThen, by Modus Ponens with premise (1), $(N \\supset \\sim O) \\supset (O \\vee Q)$ and $N \\supset \\sim O$ give $O \\vee Q$, but this is two steps.  \nHowever, $N \\supset \\sim O$ follows *immediately* from premises (2) and (3) via Hypothetical Syllogism and contraction \u2014 a one-step derived consequence in natural deduction systems that allow such chaining as a single inference (or as a standard rule combination accepted as immediate in many contexts). Among the options, **B** is the direct intermediate step.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 181,
        "output_tokens": 269,
