    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_78",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion:  Use indirect truth tables to determine whether each set of propositions is consistent. If the set is consistent, choose an option with a consistent valuation. (There may be other consistent valuations.)\n(E \u2283 F) \u2283 G\n(~E \u2228 F) \u2261 (H \u2228 I)\nI \u2283 (J \u2283 ~H)\n~G \u2283 ~J\n\nOptions: \nA. Inconsistent\nB. Consistent. Consistent valuation when E is true and F, G, H, I, and J are false\nC. Consistent. Consistent valuation when E and H are true and F, G, I, and J are false\nD. Consistent. Consistent valuation when E and I are true and F, G, H, and J are false\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine consistency, we check if there exists a truth value assignment that makes all propositions true. Testing option B: E = T, F = F, G = F, H = F, I = F, J = F. Then (E \u2283 F) = F, so (E \u2283 F) \u2283 G = T \u2283 F = F \u2014 fails. Try option C: E = T, H = T, others false. (E \u2283 F) = F, so (F \u2283 G) = T; (~E \u2228 F) = F, (H \u2228 I) = T, so biconditional is F \u2261 T = F \u2014 fails. Option D: E = T, I = T, F,G,H,J = F. (E \u2283 F) = F, so (E \u2283 F) \u2283 G = F \u2283 F = T. (~E \u2228 F) = F, (H \u2228 I) = T, so F \u2261 T = F \u2014 fails. Now test A: assume inconsistent. But try E = F, F = F, G = T, H = F, I = F, J = F. Then (E \u2283 F) = T, so (T \u2283 G) = T. (~E \u2228 F) = T, (H \u2228 I) = F, so T \u2261 F = F \u2014 fails. Try E = F, F = T, G = T, H = F, I = F, J = F. (E \u2283 F) = T, so (T \u2283 G) = T. (~E \u2228 F) = T \u2228 T = T, (H \u2228 I) = F, T \u2261 F = F. Still fails. Try I = T, H = F, J = F: I \u2283 (J \u2283 ~H) = T \u2283 (F \u2283 T) = T \u2283 T = T. After full analysis, option B works under corrected evaluation: E = T, F = F, G = F, then (E \u2283 F) = F, so (F \u2283 G) = T. (~E \u2228 F) = F \u2228 F = F, (H \u2228 I) = F \u2228 F = F, so F \u2261 F = T. I \u2283 (J \u2283 ~H): F \u2283 anything = T. ~G = T, ~J = T, so T \u2283 T = T. All true. So B is correct.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
