      "generated_answer": "To assess validity using an indirect truth table, assume the premises are true and the conclusion is false. The conclusion ~(E \u2261 F) is false when E and F have the same truth value (both true or both false). Trying E and F both true makes the first premise E \u2283 (F \u2228 ~G) true regardless of G, and the second premise F \u2283 (~E \u2228 ~G) becomes true only if ~E or ~G is true; with E true, we need ~G true, so G must be false. Thus, when E and F are true and G is false, both premises are true but the conclusion is false \u2014 a counterexample.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
