    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Use indirect truth tables to determine whether the following argument is valid. If the argument is invalid, choose an option which presents a counterexample. (There may be other counterexamples as well.)\nP \u2283 (Q \u2228 R)\n(Q \u00b7 S) \u2283 ~P\n~(~Q \u2228 R) / ~P\n\nOptions: \nA. Valid\nB. Invalid. Counterexample when P, Q, R, and S are true\nC. Invalid. Counterexample when P and Q are true and R and S are false\nD. Invalid. Counterexample when P, R, and S are true and Q is false\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We test the argument using an indirect truth table by assuming the premises are true and the conclusion false (i.e., ~P is false \u21d2 P is true), and see if this leads to a contradiction.\n\nAssume P = T (since ~P is false), then from premise 1: P \u2283 (Q \u2228 R), we get Q \u2228 R = T.  \nPremise 2: (Q \u00b7 S) \u2283 ~P, and since ~P is false, (Q \u00b7 S) must be false (to keep implication true).  \nPremise 3: ~(~Q \u2228 R) \u2261 Q \u00b7 ~R, so for this to be true, Q = T and R = F.  \nThen Q \u2228 R = T \u2228 F = T satisfies premise 1.  \n(Q \u00b7 S) = T \u00b7 S; for this to be false (to make premise 2 true), S = F.  \nSo assignment: P = T, Q = T, R = F, S = F makes all premises true and conclusion (~P) false \u2192 counterexample.\n\nThis matches option **C**.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
