eans: there does not exist a senior who graduates without a major: ~\u2203x(Sx \u2227 Gx \u2227 ~Mx). Without Mx, perhaps Gx is meant to imply graduation without major? Unclear. But standard paraphrase: \u2200x(Sx \u2192 (Gx \u2192 Mx)). Not representable. Among options, none capture Mx. So likely, the question treats \"graduates\" as \"graduates without a major\" implicitly. Then \"No senior graduates without a major\" becomes: no senior graduates (if we take \"graduates\" to mean \"graduates without major\"). Then A: \u2200x(Sx \u2283 ~Gx) would mean all seniors do not graduate \u2014 possibly intended if Gx means \"graduates without major\". That fits. So A is best under that reading.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
