    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We construct truth tables for both statements:\n\n1. $(G \\lor \\lnot H) \\supset G$:  \n   - Fails only when antecedent true and consequent false \u2192 occurs when $G = F$, $H = T$ (since $\\lnot H = F$, $G \\lor \\lnot H = F$, so implication true). Actually, check all rows:  \n     $G=T,H=T$: $(T \\lor F)=T$, $T \\supset T = T$  \n     $G=T,H=F$: $(T \\lor T)=T$, $T \\supset T = T$  \n     $G=F,H=T$: $(F \\lor F)=F$, $F \\supset F = T$  \n     $G=F,H=F$: $(F \\lor T)=T$, $T \\supset F = F$  \n     So truth values: T, T, T, F  \n\n2. $\\lnot G \\equiv (\\lnot H \\cdot G)$:  \n   Compute $\\lnot H \\cdot G$: only true if $H=F$ and $G=T$ \u2192 row 2.  \n   $\\lnot G$ true when $G=F$. So biconditional true when both sides same truth value:  \n     Row 1 ($G=T,H=T$): $\\lnot G = F$, $\\lnot H \\cdot G = F \\cdot T = F$ \u2192 T  \n     Row 2 ($G=T,H=F$): $\\lnot G = F$, $\\lnot H \\cdot G = T \\cdot T = T$ \u2192 F  \n     Row 3 ($G=F,H=T$): $\\lnot G = T$, $\\lnot H \\cdot G = F \\cdot F = F$ \u2192 F  \n     Row 4 ($G=F,H=F$): $\\lnot G = T$, $\\lnot H \\cdot G = T \\cdot F = F$ \u2192 F  \n     Truth values: T, F, F, F  \n\nTruth values differ \u2192 not equivalent. No row where both true \u2192 not consistent. One true and one false in all rows? No\u2014row 1 both T? Wait: first stmt row1 = T, second = T \u2192 both true \u2192 consistent. But \u201ccontradictory\u201d means always opposite truth values \u2192 not the case. So neither equivalent nor contradictory, but consistent.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
