      "generated_answer": "Constructing truth tables:\n\nFor I \u2261 ~H:\n- When H = T, I = F \u2192 I \u2261 ~H = T  \n- When H = F, I = T \u2192 I \u2261 ~H = T  \nSo I \u2261 ~H is always true (tautology).\n\nFor ~(~I \u2261 H):\n~I \u2261 H is false when ~I and H differ; negating gives truth when they differ.\n- H = T: ~I \u2261 T \u21d2 I = F; then ~(F \u2261 T) = ~F = T  \n- H = F: ~I \u2261 F \u21d2 I = T; then ~(T \u2261 F) = ~F = T  \nSo ~(~I \u2261 H) is also always true.\n\nBoth statements are tautologies \u21d2 logically equivalent.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
