    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Constructing the truth tables for ~(J \u2228 K) \u00b7 L and (L \u2283 J) \u00b7 K reveals no row where both are true (so not consistent), and no row where both are false (so not contradictory). However, checking rows where either is true: when J=F, K=F, L=T, first statement is true, second is false; when J=T, K=T, L=T, second is true, first is false. Since there is no row where both are true, they are not consistent. But since there is at least one row where each is true (separately), they are not contradictory. Wait\u2014contradictory requires opposite truth values in *every* row. Here, when J=T, K=F, L=F: first is F, second is F; same value \u2192 not contradictory. When J=F, K=F, L=F: both false again. So not contradictory. Are they consistent? Consistent means *at least one* row where both are true\u2014none exist. So they are *inconsistent*.  \nWait\u2014recheck: J=T, K=F, L=T: first ~(T\u2228F)\u00b7T = F\u00b7T = F; second (T\u2283T)\u00b7F = T\u00b7F = F. J=F, K=T, L=T: first ~(F\u2228T)\u00b7T = F\u00b7T = F; second (T\u2283F)\u00b7T = F\u00b7T = F. Only possible candidate for both true? Try J=F, K=F, L=T: first ~(F\u2228F)\u00b7T = T\u00b7T = T; second (T\u2283F)\u00b7F = F\u00b7F = F. No row yields both true. Both never true \u2192 not consistent. Are they ever both false? Yes (e.g., J=T,K=F,L=F), so not contradictory. But \"inconsistent\" means *never both true*, which holds. So D is correct.  \n\\boxed{D}",
