    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Gresham's Law states that when two forms of money are in circulation with the same face value but different intrinsic worth, the one with lower intrinsic value (bad money) tends to be used more in transactions, while the one with higher intrinsic value (good money) is hoarded or removed from circulation. This leads to \"bad money driving out good\" over time. The law is named after Sir Thomas Gresham, a 16th-century financial advisor to the English crown.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
