      "generated_answer": "The monetary multiplier refers to how much the money supply can increase based on an initial deposit in a fractional reserve banking system. It is commonly defined as the ratio of the total change in money supply (particularly demand deposits) to the initial change in reserves, and mathematically relates to the reserve ratio. Among the options, the most accurate definition is that it represents the ratio of total demand deposits to the total reserves of the system.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
