      "generated_answer": "The coal company\u2019s right to extract coal from the land is a classic example of a profit-a-prendre, which is the right to enter another\u2019s land and remove soil, minerals, or other substances. This interest is distinct from an easement because it involves taking something from the land, not just using it. The fact that the company pays royalties and had permission from the owner supports that it is a profit, not trespass or waste.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
