    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In jurisdictions where insanity is an affirmative defense, the defendant typically has the burden of production and sometimes the burden of persuasion, depending on state law. However, the most universally accepted standard is that the defendant must present enough evidence (burden of production) to raise the issue of insanity. The prosecution retains the burden of disproving the defense beyond a reasonable doubt unless the jurisdiction places the burden of persuasion on the defendant; but the threshold requirement is generally just the burden of production.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
