      "generated_answer": "The rule of recognition functions as a criterion for identifying valid laws, primarily guiding officials like judges rather than granting them discretionary authority. While secondary rules generally confer powers, the rule of recognition mainly imposes a duty on judges to apply laws according to recognized sources, limiting its role as purely power-conferring. Thus, it is more accurate to say it imposes duties without independently conferring power.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
