      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{C}  \nThe Equal Protection Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment prohibits states from denying equal protection of the laws to any person within their jurisdiction, including non-citizen residents. While states have some authority to regulate employment, blanket citizenship requirements for public employment like teaching are subject to judicial scrutiny and often found unconstitutional unless tied to a compelling government interest. Courts have generally ruled that such requirements violate equal protection when applied to lawful resident aliens seeking teaching positions.",
