      "generated_answer": "The covenant against encumbrances in a general warranty deed guarantees the property is free from liens or claims at the time of conveyance. Under a notice-type recording statute and title theory, the unrecorded 1953 conveyance from the son to the friend did not protect the friend against prior recorded interests, but the 1936 mortgage was given by the son before he owned the property, making it void ab initio. Thus, the 1936 mortgage was invalid and could not constitute a valid encumbrance for which the farmer would be liable.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
