      "generated_answer": "We are given a cubic polynomial:\n\n$$\np(x) = x^3 + ax^2 + bx + c\n$$\n\nwith integer coefficients $a, b, c \\in \\{-20, -19, \\dots, 20\\}$, and we want to count how many such polynomials satisfy the condition: **there exists a unique integer $m \\ne 2$** such that $p(m) = p(2)$.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the condition $p(m) = p(2)$\n\nLet\u2019s define:\n$$\np(m) = p(2)\n\\Rightarrow m^3 + am^2 + bm + c = 8 + 4a + 2b + c\n$$\nSubtracting:\n$$\nm^3 - 8 + a(m^2 - 4) + b(m - 2) = 0\n\\Rightarrow (m - 2)(m^2 + 2m + 4) + a(m - 2)(m + 2) + b(m - 2) = 0\n$$\nFactor out $(m - 2)$:\n$$\n(m - 2)\\left[m^2 + 2m + 4 + a(m + 2) + b\\right] = 0\n$$\n\nSo $p(m) = p(2)$ iff $m = 2$ or the expression in brackets is zero.\n\nWe are told there is a **unique integer $m \\ne 2$** such that $p(m) = p(2)$. That means the equation:\n$$\nm^2 + 2m + 4 + a(m + 2) + b = 0\n$$\nhas exactly **one integer solution $m \\ne 2$**.\n\nLet\u2019s simplify this equation:\n$$\nm^2 + 2m + 4 + a m + 2a + b = 0\n\\Rightarrow m^2 + (2 + a)m + (4 + 2a + b) = 0\n$$\n\nLet:\n$$\nf(m) = m^2 + (a+2)m + (2a + b + 4)\n$$\n\nWe want this quadratic in $m$ to have **exactly one integer root not equal to 2**.\n\nBut wait \u2014 a quadratic can have:\n- 0 real roots,\n- 1 real root (double root),\n- 2 real roots.\n\nWe want **exactly one integer solution $m \\ne 2$**.\n\nBut \"exactly one integer root\" is tricky because quadratics either have 0, 1 (repeated), or 2 roots (counting multiplicity).\n\nSo possibilities:\n1. The quadratic has a **double root at some integer $m_0 \\ne 2$**.\n2. The quadratic has **two distinct integer roots**, but **one of them is $m = 2$**, and the other is not \u2014 so only one valid $m \\ne 2$.\n3. The quadratic has two distinct integer roots, both different from 2 \u2014 then we'd have two values of $m \\ne 2$, violating uniqueness.\n\nSo acceptable cases:\n- Case A: Double root at integer $m_0 \\ne 2$\n- Case B: Roots are $2$ and $m_1$, where $m_1$ 