    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We want $ n+2 \\mid 3(n+3)(n^2+9) $. Let $ d = n+2 $, so $ n = d-2 $. Substitute:  \n$ 3((d-2)+3)((d-2)^2 + 9) = 3(d+1)(d^2 - 4d + 4 + 9) = 3(d+1)(d^2 - 4d + 13) $.  \nWe need $ d \\mid 3(d+1)(d^2 - 4d + 13) $. Since $ \\gcd(d, d+1) = 1 $, $ d \\mid 3(d^2 - 4d + 13) $.  \nCompute $ 3(d^2 - 4d + 13) \\mod d \\Rightarrow 3(0 - 0 + 13) = 39 \\mod d $. So $ d \\mid 39 $.  \nPositive divisors of 39: $ 1, 3, 13, 39 $. Then $ n = d - 2 $ gives $ n = -1, 1, 11, 37 $. Only positive $ n $: $ 1, 11, 37 $.  \nSum: $ 1 + 11 + 37 = 49 $.\n\n\\boxed{{49}}",
