) = \\frac{1}{2}(1 + E(0,1)) + \\frac{1}{2}(0 + E(1,0)) = \\frac{1}{2}(1+1) + \\frac{1}{2}(0+1) = 1 + 0.5 = 1.5 $? But $ E(0,1)=1 $, $ E(1,0)=1 $, so $ \\frac{1}{2}(1+1) + \\frac{1}{2}(0+1) = 1 + 0.5 = 1.5 $. But maximum possible is 2, and optimal should do better? No: with 1R1B, you guess (say) red first: 50% right, then know second. So expected: 0.5\u00d7(1+1) + 0.5\u00d7(0+1) = 0.5\u00d72 + 0.5\u00d71 = 1 + 0.5 = 1.5. Yes.\n\nNow $ E(2,1) $: since r>b, guess red.\n$ E(2,1) = \\frac{2}{3}(1 + E(1,1)) + \\frac{1}{3}(0 + E(2,0)) $\n$ E(1,1)=1.5 $, $ E(2,0)=2 $\nSo $ = \\frac{2}{3}(1 + 1.5) + \\frac{1}{3}(0 + 2) = \\frac{2}{3}(2.5) + \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{5}{3} + \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{7}{3} \\approx 2.333 $? Wait: $ \\frac{2}{3} \\times 2.5 = \\frac{2}{3} \\times \\frac{5}{2} = \\frac{5}{3} \\approx 1.666 $, plus $ \\frac{1}{3} \\times 2 = \\frac{2}{3} \\approx 0.666 $, total $ \\frac{7}{3} \\approx 2.333 $. But max is 3, ok.\n\nSimilarly, $ E(1,2) = E(2,1) = \\frac{7}{3} $ by symmetry.\n\nNow $ E(2,2) $: r=b, so guess red (wlog).\n$ E(2,2) = \\frac{2}{4}(1 + E(1,2)) + \\frac{2}{4}(0 + E(2,1)) = \\frac{1}{2}(1 + \\frac{7}{3}) + \\frac{1}{2}(0 + \\frac{7}{3}) = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{10}{3} + \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{7}{3} = \\frac{10}{6} + \\frac{7}{6} = \\frac{17}{6} \\approx 2.833 $\n\nNow $ E(3,1) $: r>b, guess red.\n$ E(3,1) = \\frac{3}{4}(1 + E(2,1)) + \\frac{1}{4}(0 + E(3,0)) = \\frac{3}{4}(1 + \\frac{7}{3}) + \\frac{1}{4}(0 + 3) = \\frac{3}{4} \\cdot \\frac{10}{3} + \\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{10}{4} + \\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{13}{4} = 3.25 $\n\n$ E(1,3) = \\frac{13}{4} $\n\nNow $ E(3,2) $: r>b, guess red.\n$ E(3,2) = \\frac{3}{5}(1 + E(2,2)) + \\frac{2}{5}(0 + E(3,1)) = \\frac{3}{5}(1 + \\frac{17}{6}) + \\frac{2}{5}(0 + \\frac{13}{4}) $\nCompute: $ 1 + \\frac{17}{6} = \\frac{23}{6} $, so $ \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{23}{6} = \\frac{69}{30} = \\frac{23}{10} $\n$ \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{13}{4} = \\frac{26}{20} = \\frac{13}{10} $\nSum: $ \\frac{23}{10} + \\frac{13}{10} = \\frac{36}{10} = 3.6 $\n\nSimilarly, $ E(2,3) = E(3,2) = \\frac{36}{10} = \\frac{18}{5} $\n\nFinally, $ E(3,3) $: r=b, guess red.\n$ E(3,3) = \\frac{3}{6}(1 + E(2,3)) + \\frac{3}{6}(0 + E(3,2)) = \\frac{1}{2}(1 + \\frac{18}{5}) + \\frac{1}{2}(0 + \\frac{18}{5}) $\n$ = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{23}{5} + \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{18}{5} = \\frac{23}{10} + \\frac{18}{10} = \\frac{41}{10} $\n\nSo expected correct guesses is $ \\frac{41}{10} $, m=41, n=10, relatively prime, so m+n=51.\n\n\\boxed{51}",
