    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Using l'H\u00f4pital's rule twice, we differentiate numerator and denominator to get the limit as $-1/2$. The Taylor series of $\\ln(1+x)$ is $x - x^2/2 + x^3/3 - \\cdots$, so $\\ln(1+x) - x = -x^2/2 + x^3/3 - \\cdots$, and dividing by $x^2$ gives $-1/2 + x/3 - \\cdots$, which tends to $-1/2$ as $x \\to 0$. Both methods confirm the result.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
