    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Find $\\gamma$ so that the solution of the initial value problem $x^2 y^{{\\prime \\prime}}-2 y=0, y(1)=1, y^{{\\prime}}(1)=\\gamma$ is bounded as $x \\rightarrow 0$.\n\nOptions: \nA. 6\nB. 5\nC. 1/2\nD. 1\nE. 0\nF. 3\nG.  2\nH. -2\nI. -1\nJ. 4\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The differential equation $ x^2 y'' - 2y = 0 $ is a Cauchy-Euler equation. Assuming a solution of the form $ y = x^r $, we get the characteristic equation $ r(r-1) - 2 = 0 $, which simplifies to $ r^2 - r - 2 = 0 $. Solving gives roots $ r = 2 $ and $ r = -1 $, so the general solution is $ y = C_1 x^2 + C_2 x^{-1} $.\n\nFor $ y $ to be bounded as $ x \\to 0 $, the coefficient of $ x^{-1} $ must be zero, so $ C_2 = 0 $. Then $ y = C_1 x^2 $, and $ y(1) = 1 $ implies $ C_1 = 1 $. Thus, $ y = x^2 $, so $ y' = 2x $, and $ y'(1) = 2 $. Therefore, $ \\gamma = 2 $.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
