      "generated_answer": "The random variables $ X_k $ are independent with $ \\mathbb{E}[X_k] = 0 $ and $ \\text{Var}(X_k) = 1 $. The sum $ S_n = \\sum_{k=1}^n k X_k $ has mean 0 and variance $ \\sum_{k=1}^n k^2 = \\frac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}{6} \\sim \\frac{n^3}{3} $. Then $ \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{n^3}} S_n $ has variance asymptotically $ \\frac{3}{n^3} \\cdot \\frac{n^3}{3} = 1 $, so it converges to $ N(0,1) $.\n\nThus, $ a = 0 $, $ b = 1 $.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
