    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Suppose $x-3$ and $y+3$ are multiples of $7$. What is the smallest positive integer, $n,$ for which $x^2+xy+y^2+n$ is a multiple of $7$?\n\nOptions: \nA. 9\nB. 8\nC. 5\nD. 6\nE. 3\nF. 2\nG. 4\nH. 7\nI. 10\nJ. 1\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Since $x - 3 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{7}$ and $y + 3 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{7}$, we have $x \\equiv 3 \\pmod{7}$ and $y \\equiv -3 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{7}$. Then compute $x^2 + xy + y^2 + n \\pmod{7}$:  \n$x^2 \\equiv 9$, $xy \\equiv 3 \\cdot 4 = 12$, $y^2 \\equiv 16$, so sum is $9 + 12 + 16 = 37 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7}$. Thus, $2 + n \\equiv 0 \\pmod{7}$ \u2192 $n \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7}$. Smallest positive $n$ is 5.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
