    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Statement 1 is true: if a set $ A $ is connected, its closure is also connected.  \nStatement 2 is true: by Brouwer's fixed-point theorem applied to continuous functions on compact intervals, or via intermediate value theorem arguments, every continuous $ f: [0,1] \\to [0,1] $ has a fixed point; though $ (0,1) $ is not closed, one can extend continuously to $ [0,1] $ and still guarantee a fixed point in $ (0,1) $ under certain conditions \u2014 but actually, counterexamples exist (e.g., $ f(x) = x^2 $), wait \u2014 no, $ f(x) = x^2 $ has fixed points at 0 and 1, not in $ (0,1) $. Actually, consider $ f(x) = x/2 $, which maps $ (0,1) \\to (0,1) $ and only fixes 0, not in domain. So no fixed point in $ (0,1) $. Thus, Statement 2 is false.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
