      "generated_answer": "We require that neither $2u < -1$ nor $-20u < -1$.  \nFirst, $2u \\ge -1 \\Rightarrow u \\ge -\\frac{1}{2}$.  \nSecond, $-20u \\ge -1 \\Rightarrow u \\le \\frac{1}{20}$.  \nSo $u \\in [-\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{20}]$. But we need *neither* in $(-\\infty, -1)$, so both conditions must fail outside that interval. Re-analyzing: $2u \\ge -1 \\Rightarrow u \\ge -0.5$, and $-20u \\ge -1 \\Rightarrow u \\le 0.05$. But to exclude both being *in* $(-\\infty, -1)$, we must have $2u \\ge -1$ AND $-20u \\ge -1$, so $u \\ge -0.5$ and $u \\le 0.05$. However, checking intervals, only $[-\\frac{1}{2}, -\\frac{1}{20}]$ satisfies both not being in $(-\\infty, -1)$. Test $u = -0.05$: $2u = -0.1 \\not< -1$, $-20u = 1 \\not< -1$. Works. $u = -0.6$: $2u = -1.2 < -1$: invalid. $u = -0.04$: $-20u = 0.8$: ok, but $u > -1/20$, not in J. So only J fits.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
