    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To synchronize two clocks separated by a distance $ d $, one must account for the finite speed of light $ c $. The clock at distance $ d $ should be set ahead by the time it takes light to travel from the origin to that location, so it reads the current time at the origin when the signal arrives. This means the distant clock should be pre-set to the current time plus $ d/c $, but since we are sending a signal from the origin, we subtract the travel time from the reading at emission.\n\nThus, the correct answer is that the distant clock should be pre-set to the current time minus $ d/c $, so that when the synchronization signal (traveling at $ c $) arrives, both clocks read the same.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
